    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2025/06/asics-metaspeed-sky-tokyo-review-2025.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning is plush and impact-protective, with a very soft, bouncy heel and a firmer, snappier forefoot; overall cushioning is sufficient for marathon distance.",
        "Responsiveness is high: the FF Leap/FF Turbo+ setup and carbon plate deliver strong bounce and energy return, with the forefoot feeling especially structured and quick at toe-off.",
        "Stability is mixed: a wider heel platform, sole flare, stiff plated build, and secure upper improve support, but the highly compliant heel (with a minimally wrapping heel counter) can feel unstable, especially in sharp turns.",
        "Durability appears average-to-good: ASICS Grip outsole traction is strong and reported to last about 200-300 miles, though medial heel compression marks suggest the soft foam may show wear sooner in heavy heel strikers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2025/06/asics-metaspeed-sky-tokyo-review-3.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The layered midsole (soft FF LEAP under the plate, firmer FF Turbo Plus above) feels very soft and bouncy, with the heel softer and the forefoot firmer; the 39.5 mm heel / 34.5 mm forefoot stack suggests strong impact protection.",
        "Responsiveness: Testers describe a fast, snappy, propulsive ride; ASICS claims FF LEAP is 13.7% more responsive and Sky Tokyo is 18.8% more energy efficient than Sky Paris, aided by a higher, flatter carbon plate for vertical propulsion.",
        "Stability: Reviewers call it more stable than Sky Paris, with secure lockdown and lateral support from the upper despite minimal structure; some heel lift was noted but improved using the extra eyelet/runner’s knot.",
        "Durability: Traction is highlighted as very good for a super shoe, but the provided text has limited long-term evidence on outsole wear and material longevity."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/asics-metaspeed-sky-tokyo-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The dual-density setup (FF Leap under FF Turbo Plus) gives a soft, high-stack, protective ride, though the Sky Tokyo is characterized as firmer/snappier than earlier Sky versions, especially up front.",
        "Responsiveness: The plate positioned close to the foot plus the new bouncy foam produces strong energy return and a clearly propulsive, quick-feeling toe-off.",
        "Stability: It is reported to feel less stable at slower paces, with some heel lift and a lightweight upper that prioritizes low weight over maximal structure/lateral support.",
        "Durability: The AsicsGrip outsole is described as a proven rubber suitable for many conditions, but the provided text does not include long-term wear testing for outsole or upper longevity."
      ]
    }
